1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
Do you want peace?

2
00:00:01,801 --> 00:00:03,381
Then prepare for war.

3
00:00:03,824 --> 00:00:05,504
It sounds almost absurd.

4
00:00:06,284 --> 00:00:10,024
If the goal is never to fight, why
build tanks, missiles and warships?

5
00:00:10,598 --> 00:00:13,698
And yet, in 2025, the
world spent nearly two

6
00:00:13,928 --> 00:00:16,318
point nine trillion
dollars on the military.

7
00:00:16,898 --> 00:00:18,718
The most ever recorded.

8
00:00:19,485 --> 00:00:22,325
Most governments that spend
it say the same thing.

9
00:00:22,965 --> 00:00:24,725
It's not for starting a war.

10
00:00:25,025 --> 00:00:26,545
It's for preventing one.

11
00:00:26,916 --> 00:00:28,516
So is that a contradiction?

12
00:00:29,396 --> 00:00:33,316
Or is preparing for war one of the
reasons war sometimes doesn't happen?

13
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:38,766
Start with the simplest
version. Two countries.

14
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:41,426
One is thinking about attacking the other.

15
00:00:41,786 --> 00:00:47,246
If the target is weak, the attack looks
cheap. A few weeks. A quick victory. Done.

16
00:00:47,913 --> 00:00:49,453
Now change one thing.

17
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:53,553
The target can fight back, and make
the war long, bloody, expensive.

18
00:00:54,653 --> 00:00:56,353
The attacker's question changes.

19
00:00:56,653 --> 00:00:58,613
It's no longer "can we win?"

20
00:00:58,693 --> 00:01:01,093
It's "is winning worth the price?"

21
00:01:01,777 --> 00:01:04,857
That's deterrence. You don't
have to defeat your enemy.

22
00:01:05,437 --> 00:01:08,877
You have to convince them that
attacking you is a terrible deal.

23
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:13,007
The idea is old.

24
00:01:13,507 --> 00:01:17,147
Its famous Latin version is
si vis pacem, para bellum.

25
00:01:17,631 --> 00:01:20,751
It's usually credited
to Vegetius, a Roman who

26
00:01:20,981 --> 00:01:23,991
wrote about warfare about
sixteen hundred years ago.

27
00:01:24,553 --> 00:01:26,773
He never wrote those exact words.

28
00:01:27,373 --> 00:01:31,653
What he wrote was longer: let him
who desires peace prepare for war.

29
00:01:32,246 --> 00:01:34,046
It wasn't a boast, either.

30
00:01:34,626 --> 00:01:37,866
His book was a complaint that
Rome had let its army decay.

31
00:01:38,546 --> 00:01:41,306
Soldiers, he wrote, had
stopped drilling, and even

32
00:01:41,536 --> 00:01:44,026
stopped wearing their
helmets and body armour.

33
00:01:44,634 --> 00:01:48,114
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

34
00:01:48,734 --> 00:01:50,354
It really helps the channel.

35
00:01:52,724 --> 00:01:54,524
Weakness invites attack.

36
00:01:55,164 --> 00:01:58,884
That part of the idea holds up
well. There's a catch, though.

37
00:01:58,964 --> 00:02:01,544
Your enemy can see your preparations too.

38
00:02:02,125 --> 00:02:05,825
Say Country A is worried about
Country B, so it builds more tanks.

39
00:02:06,565 --> 00:02:08,745
From the inside, that's pure defence.

40
00:02:09,541 --> 00:02:13,701
Country B can't see inside A's
head. It only sees the tanks.

41
00:02:13,781 --> 00:02:15,581
So it builds tanks of its own.

42
00:02:16,081 --> 00:02:20,381
Now A is worried again.
More tanks. Then B. Then A.

43
00:02:20,971 --> 00:02:22,851
Neither side wants a war.

44
00:02:23,231 --> 00:02:24,951
Both think they're only defending.

45
00:02:25,591 --> 00:02:28,211
Both end up more armed, and less safe.

46
00:02:28,921 --> 00:02:34,681
In 1950, the political scientist John Herz
gave this a name: the security dilemma.

47
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:43,421
It isn't just theory. In 1906, Britain
launched HMS Dreadnought, a battleship

48
00:02:43,651 --> 00:02:46,221
so advanced it made
every other one outdated.

49
00:02:46,638 --> 00:02:48,278
Including Britain's own.

50
00:02:48,898 --> 00:02:52,498
Its huge lead in older ships
suddenly counted for much less.

51
00:02:53,398 --> 00:02:57,118
Germany, already building a fleet,
raced to match the new design.

52
00:02:57,738 --> 00:03:00,118
Each side said it was only for defence.

53
00:03:00,905 --> 00:03:03,985
The race didn't cause the
First World War on its own.

54
00:03:04,465 --> 00:03:06,445
It certainly didn't prevent it.

55
00:03:09,015 --> 00:03:12,615
Then came nuclear weapons, and
the logic got even stranger.

56
00:03:13,535 --> 00:03:18,795
At the peak, in 1986, the world had
around seventy thousand nuclear warheads.

57
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:21,300
Almost all of them belonged to just two

58
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:25,020
countries: the United
States and the Soviet Union.

59
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:29,080
Far more than either side
needed to devastate the other.

60
00:03:29,649 --> 00:03:31,929
Why would that make anyone safer?

61
00:03:32,825 --> 00:03:36,805
Because even after a first strike, the
other side could still strike back.

62
00:03:37,505 --> 00:03:39,165
Attacking would be suicide.

63
00:03:39,848 --> 00:03:44,828
It became known as mutually assured
destruction. Or, fittingly, MAD.

64
00:03:45,228 --> 00:03:48,208
I won't destroy you,
because you can destroy me.

65
00:03:48,448 --> 00:03:51,028
You won't destroy me, for the same reason.

66
00:03:51,619 --> 00:03:54,639
The weapons weren't useful
because they could be used.

67
00:03:55,259 --> 00:03:58,059
They were useful because using
them would be a catastrophe.

68
00:03:59,213 --> 00:04:01,713
Which gives military
power a strange property.

69
00:04:02,493 --> 00:04:08,813
When it works, nothing happens. No battle.
No victory. No headline. Just... nothing.

70
00:04:09,406 --> 00:04:15,446
Since 1949, no outside power has invaded
the territory the NATO treaty protects.

71
00:04:16,243 --> 00:04:20,643
Is that deterrence? Or would nobody
have tried anyway? We can't know.

72
00:04:20,723 --> 00:04:22,723
History doesn't let us run it twice.

73
00:04:23,443 --> 00:04:25,643
We only ever see the wars that happened.

74
00:04:28,133 --> 00:04:30,153
Deterrence does fail, though.

75
00:04:30,593 --> 00:04:32,813
It doesn't depend on weapons alone.

76
00:04:33,453 --> 00:04:35,793
It depends on what the
other side believes.

77
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:40,020
In 1981, Britain announced
it would retire its

78
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:42,820
only regular warship
in the South Atlantic.

79
00:04:43,561 --> 00:04:45,301
It was meant to save money.

80
00:04:46,021 --> 00:04:50,461
In Argentina, it was read as one more
sign that Britain was losing interest.

81
00:04:51,033 --> 00:04:53,613
The ship itself couldn't
have stopped an invasion.

82
00:04:54,413 --> 00:04:57,333
What mattered was what
withdrawing it seemed to say.

83
00:04:58,157 --> 00:05:01,097
In 1982, Argentina invaded.

84
00:05:01,923 --> 00:05:03,863
Britain had a powerful navy.

85
00:05:04,823 --> 00:05:07,803
Argentina's own inquiry later
found that the junta had

86
00:05:08,033 --> 00:05:11,043
thought a British military
response almost impossible.

87
00:05:11,791 --> 00:05:13,651
Deterrence needs two things.

88
00:05:14,131 --> 00:05:17,451
The power to strike back, and
an opponent convinced you will.

89
00:05:17,711 --> 00:05:21,071
Both sides are judging that
with incomplete information.

90
00:05:21,211 --> 00:05:23,891
Sometimes, in a matter of minutes.

91
00:05:26,481 --> 00:05:30,001
In September 1983, a
Soviet early-warning system

92
00:05:30,231 --> 00:05:35,361
reported a missile launched from
the United States. Then four more.

93
00:05:35,987 --> 00:05:41,207
The officer on duty, Stanislav Petrov,
had to judge whether the warning was real.

94
00:05:41,927 --> 00:05:44,047
He reported it as a false alarm.

95
00:05:44,619 --> 00:05:45,819
He was right.

96
00:05:46,099 --> 00:05:49,979
A satellite had mistaken sunlight
off high clouds for rocket engines.

97
00:05:50,797 --> 00:05:53,797
A machine built to remove
all doubt had got it wrong.

98
00:05:54,377 --> 00:05:56,557
It took a human's doubt to say so.

99
00:05:59,107 --> 00:06:01,867
That leaves countries
walking a narrow line.

100
00:06:02,387 --> 00:06:04,967
Strong enough that attacking
them looks irrational.

101
00:06:05,887 --> 00:06:09,167
Not so threatening that a
neighbour decides to strike first.

102
00:06:09,809 --> 00:06:11,969
From the outside, defensive and offensive

103
00:06:12,199 --> 00:06:15,469
preparations can look
almost exactly the same.

104
00:06:17,959 --> 00:06:23,779
After the Cuban Missile Crisis in 1962,
the two superpowers built something new.

105
00:06:24,149 --> 00:06:28,189
Not a weapon. A direct line
between Washington and Moscow.

106
00:06:29,129 --> 00:06:31,189
During the crisis, one Soviet letter took

107
00:06:31,419 --> 00:06:33,869
nearly twelve hours to
reach the White House.

108
00:06:34,229 --> 00:06:37,669
In 1963, the hotline went live.

109
00:06:38,352 --> 00:06:40,352
It wasn't the famous red telephone.

110
00:06:40,832 --> 00:06:42,532
It was a teleprinter.

111
00:06:43,252 --> 00:06:46,512
It meant the two sides could
talk before anyone had to shoot.

112
00:06:49,012 --> 00:06:51,532
So, does preparing for war keep the peace?

113
00:06:52,844 --> 00:06:54,264
Sometimes, yes.

114
00:06:54,624 --> 00:06:59,044
A country that can defend itself is
harder to bully, and harder to invade.

115
00:06:59,854 --> 00:07:02,374
Weapons don't create peace on their own.

116
00:07:02,914 --> 00:07:07,154
They also create fear, arms races,
and room for catastrophic mistakes.

117
00:07:07,973 --> 00:07:10,213
Deterrence only works when
the other side believes

118
00:07:10,443 --> 00:07:13,713
the threat, understands
it, and doesn't panic.

119
00:07:14,448 --> 00:07:17,588
The best deterrent may be a
weapon that is never fired.

120
00:07:18,268 --> 00:07:21,228
And the proof that it worked
may be nothing happening at all.

121
00:07:23,288 --> 00:07:24,488
Do you want peace?

122
00:07:25,089 --> 00:07:26,669
Then prepare for war.

123
00:07:27,512 --> 00:07:29,942
But perhaps... prepare
even harder for peace.
